message.content:
Below are structured extractions from 14 posts on blog.daana.dev, produced by agents that each read one post in full.

Four further posts were read separately and are NOT in this set (do not re-derive them, but you may reference them): "The Design of Analytical Data Storage Systems" (the three-layer DAS/DAB/DAR architecture + Ensemble Modeling), "From Business Question to Working Prototype in Hours" (the 5-step Agile Analytics methodology), "Contract-Driven Data Transformation" (YAML contracts generating staging SQL in DAS), "The Anatomy of a Business Question" (the [AGGREGATION] of [MEASURE] per [DIMENSION] form, stages 0-3).

<extractions>
[
  {
    "slug": "the-architectural-blueprint",
    "title": "The Architectural Blueprint",
    "thesis": "An architectural blueprint is the overarching, technology-free vision document for a Data Warehouse: it states what the system must achieve (maintainable, scalable, adaptable, good time to market, over a 15-25 year life cycle) and the absolute logical principles that support that vision, with no mention of technology, tooling, process, or modeling method. Its concrete content here is a three-layer logical architecture — DAS (raw, as delivered by the source system), DAB (source-agnostic business semantics), DAR (per-requirement, per-user-group data products) — plus a strict one-directional flow (source → DAS → DAB → DAR → consumers) and a table of per-layer principles stated as absolutes, each with a documented \"Why It Matters\". The blueprint deliberately contains no exceptions: its job is to act as a gatekeeper that forces an informed, costed decision every time a requirement wants to break a principle, rather than to pre-authorize deviations. It names no technologies, files, commands, schemas or column conventions — so it constrains what DAS/DAB/DAR may contain and how data may move between them, not how to implement them.",
    "relevance": "high",
    "mandates": [
      {
        "claim": "\"Data usage for analytical purposes is only allowed from the DAR layer.\" Reason given: \"All analytical consumption goes through DAR so DAB remains a stable, reusable foundation. If users query DAB directly, their usage patterns create implicit dependencies that make the system harder to change.\"",
        "layer": "cross-cutting",
        "implication": "The generated Unified Star Schema in DAR must be the only surface exposed to BI tools, notebooks, ad-hoc SQL and any analytical consumer. No end-user or dashboard may be pointed at DAS or at the DAB focal model, however convenient — access must be arranged so DAB has no analytical consumers creating implicit dependencies."
      },
      {
        "claim": "\"Data in the DAR layer can only come from the DAB layer.\" Reason given: \"Every analytical product must be built on business-integrated, quality-assured data. Bypassing DAB means bypassing the single source of truth, leading to inconsistent numbers and eroded trust.\"",
        "layer": "DAR",
        "implication": "Every DAR/USS model must select exclusively from DAB relations. No DAR object may read a DAS table, a source system, or a file drop — not even to 'enrich' or 'fill a gap'. A missing attribute in a DAR product is a reason to add it to DAB, never a reason to reach past DAB."
      },
      {
        "claim": "\"Data in the DAB layer can only come from the DAS layer.\" Reason given: \"Clean separation between raw data capture and business integration. DAS absorbs all source-system volatility, so changes in data delivery never force changes in business logic.\"",
        "layer": "DAB",
        "implication": "The DAB focal model built with daana-cli must source only from DAS tables. No source connection, API call or external lookup may be wired directly into DAB; anything DAB needs must first be landed in DAS by the ingestion layer."
      },
      {
        "claim": "DAS principle 'Persist': \"Non-volatile. Not allowed to update or delete data.\" Why It Matters: \"The data has to be auditable and the system must be able to reload downstream layers with all historical data at any time.\"",
        "layer": "DAS",
        "implication": "dlt ingestion must be append-only. Write dispositions that overwrite or delete (replace, or a merge that physically updates/removes rows) violate the principle; every load must be retained so DAB and DAR can be rebuilt from full DAS history at any time."
      },
      {
        "claim": "DAS principle 'Simplicity': \"Capture and persist only. Transformation logic resides in the code that loads DAB.\" Why It Matters: \"A simple ingestion layer means new sources can be onboarded with minimal effort.\"",
        "layer": "DAS",
        "implication": "No cleaning, renaming, casting, deduplication, filtering, joining or business logic in the dlt pipelines — capture the source's raw format as delivered. All transformation belongs in the DAS→DAB code. Onboarding a new source must stay close to a configuration exercise."
      },
      {
        "claim": "DAS principle 'Metadata': \"Every data point entering DAS has to be recorded for auditability.\" Why It Matters: \"Always have the ability to see from where and when data came into the system.\"",
        "layer": "DAS",
        "implication": "Every landed record must carry (or be joinable to) provenance metadata answering from where and when it entered. Load-level audit records alone are insufficient if a single data point cannot be traced back to its origin and arrival time."
      },
      {
        "claim": "DAB principle 'Persist': \"Non-volatile. Not allowed to update or delete data.\" Why It Matters: \"Business-integrated data has to be auditable and traceable over time. Immutability ensures we can reproduce any analytical result and understand how business data evolved.\"",
        "layer": "DAB",
        "implication": "The DAB focal model must be historized and append-only — no in-place updates, no overwriting of current state, no hard deletes. Any analytical number ever served must be reproducible from DAB as it stood at that time."
      },
      {
        "claim": "DAB principle 'Reusability': \"Produce clear, well-defined atomic business semantics without focus on analytical use-case.\" Why It Matters: \"Straightforward business definitions keep the layer focused on the ability to support any use case.\"",
        "layer": "DAB",
        "implication": "Focal-model entities and their definitions must be justifiable without reference to any report, dashboard or ML model. If a DAB construct only makes sense because of one downstream requirement, it belongs in DAR instead."
      },
      {
        "claim": "DAB principle 'Atomic': \"Always has the atomic data point as the base of its semantic meaning.\" Why It Matters: \"Data has to be reusable, independent of analytical use-case. Aggregations happen when data moves to DAR.\"",
        "layer": "DAB",
        "implication": "No aggregation, summarization, bucketing or pre-rollup anywhere in DAB; aggregates are computed only in the DAB→DAR step. The post explicitly names a non-atomic DAB as one of the two root causes of teams demanding raw data instead."
      },
      {
        "claim": "DAB principle 'Business Oriented': \"No semantic or structural representation that is source-system specific or use-case specific. Data has a business representation independent of both.\" Why It Matters: \"The system has to adapt to change from both sourcing and usage perspectives. DAB buffers against changes in either from cascading to other layers.\"",
        "layer": "DAB",
        "implication": "Names and structures in the focal model must be business terms, not source table/column names, source-system prefixes, source keys, or report/use-case names. Adding a second source for the same concept must not add a parallel structure; swapping a source system out must not change any DAB structure."
      },
      {
        "claim": "DAB principle 'Metadata': \"Every data point entering DAB has to be recorded for auditability.\" Why It Matters: \"Always have the ability to trace data lineage from source through integration, to understand what business rules were applied and when.\"",
        "layer": "DAB",
        "implication": "Lineage must be recorded per data point through the integration step, including which business rules were applied and when — not merely which DAS table it came from. Rule versions and effective times must be recoverable."
      },
      {
        "claim": "DAR principle 'Persist': \"Unlike DAS and DAB, DAR structures may be volatile where the requirement demands it (e.g. full refreshes of aggregated views), but all changes must be traceable.\" Why It Matters: \"Reproducibility is essential. Users and auditors need to understand what data was served, when, and how it was derived from DAB.\"",
        "layer": "DAR",
        "implication": "DAR is the only layer permitted to be rebuilt or full-refreshed — a generated USS may be dropped and regenerated. But the platform must still record what was served, when, and how it was derived from DAB, so a past delivery can be explained and (given immutable DAB) reproduced."
      },
      {
        "claim": "DAR principle 'Specialization': \"Data structures and data points are specialized to support the specific requirement(s).\" Why It Matters: \"Data should be easy to use, shaped exactly to what each user group needs.\"",
        "layer": "DAR",
        "implication": "Each generated USS is shaped to one requirement set — containing only the data points that requirement needs, in the shape it needs. A general-purpose 'one big model for everyone' in DAR violates the principle."
      },
      {
        "claim": "DAR principle 'Requirement Oriented': \"Contains the exact semantic and structural representation for specific requirement(s). No \"reuse\" between different requirements. Different user groups must not share structures even when requirements align. No intra-dependencies between requirements of different user groups.\" Why It Matters: \"Reusability in DAB ensures ease of creating unique analytical structures. DAR therefore focuses on specialization per user group, ensuring no contention between requirements. This supports good time to market throughout the system's life cycle.\"",
        "layer": "DAR",
        "implication": "Generate a separate USS per user group / requirement even when two groups would produce identical tables — deliberate duplication is required, not a smell. Do not factor out shared conformed dimensions or shared intermediate models across user groups, and never let one group's DAR object depend on another group's DAR object. Reuse belongs in DAB only."
      },
      {
        "claim": "DAR principle 'Metadata': \"Every data point entering DAR has to be recorded for auditability.\" Why It Matters: \"Always have the ability to see what data was delivered to which user group, when, and how it was derived from the business-integrated layer.\"",
        "layer": "DAR",
        "implication": "The USS generator must emit metadata tying each delivered data point to a user group, a time, and its DAB derivation — delivery-side audit, not just build logs."
      },
      {
        "claim": "\"The three layers exist to: Break down complexity in code, so data movement between layers has a specific purpose and does not try to do too many things at once. Limit the effects of changes. Each layer's code will not affect the other layers. Specialize each layer so the system avoids sub-optimization.\"",
        "layer": "cross-cutting",
        "implication": "Each inter-layer movement is one job: ingest (source→DAS), integrate (DAS→DAB), specialize (DAB→DAR). Code that ingests-and-integrates, or integrates-and-shapes-for-a-report, collapses layers and is disallowed. Layer code must be separable enough that a change in one layer's code does not force edits in another."
      },
      {
        "claim": "The layer names are chosen deliberately: \"DAS/DAB/DAR map roughly to staging/core/marts in dbt terminology, or bronze/silver/gold in the medallion architecture. The concepts overlap, but the naming here is deliberate -- it emphasizes the purpose of each layer (what the data represents) rather than its position in a sequence or a quality grade. That distinction matters when the names are also your principles.\"",
        "layer": "cross-cutting",
        "implication": "Name the schemas/layers DAS, DAB, DAR (what the data represents) rather than bronze/silver/gold or staging/core/marts — the name is meant to carry the principle. Treating them as mere renames of medallion tiers loses the point."
      },
      {
        "claim": "The blueprint states absolutes with reasons and must not contain exceptions: \"But if we do not have an absolute principle that is described -- why we have it, what effect it has -- we have nothing to break.\" And: \"Since each \"exception\" breaks the fundamental principles, it builds a less good system from a maintainability, scalability, and adaptability point of view. Time to market erodes as more exceptions are deployed, and the system's lifetime gets shorter.\"",
        "layer": "process",
        "implication": "Write each platform principle as an absolute with an explicit 'Why It Matters', and do not build configurable escape hatches, exception flags or 'bypass DAB' modes into the platform or its documentation. Deviations are handled case-by-case as costed, informed decisions — never pre-authorized in code or docs."
      },
      {
        "claim": "There is no ML/AI raw-data exception: \"Machine Learning and AI thrive on well-defined and integrated data, where quality is high and the ML/AI models get a picture of how the company works, not how one system works.\" The recommended path is \"adding the missing data to DAB and building our solution in DAR\", because \"the ML team ends up re-creating integration work that DAB already handles, but without the benefit of cross-domain consistency or reuse.\"",
        "layer": "cross-cutting",
        "implication": "No feature store or ML pipeline may read DAS. ML/AI consumes DAR like every other requirement; missing inputs are added to DAB first. Do not add an ML carve-out to the architecture document or the platform."
      },
      {
        "claim": "The blueprint is technology-free and overarching: \"It contains nothing about technology, processes, modeling methods, or design patterns.\" \"This is not the only architectural document -- it is the overarching one. From it, other documents spawn covering technology, tooling, processes, modeling techniques, and so on. But they should always support the vision statement.\"",
        "layer": "tooling",
        "implication": "Tool choices (dlt, daana-cli, the Unified Star Schema, the warehouse engine) belong in subordinate documents, and each must be justified as supporting the vision statement — none may be written into the blueprint itself, and any of them must be replaceable without invalidating it."
      },
      {
        "claim": "The vision the architecture must serve: \"The Data Warehouse has to be Maintainable, Scalable, Adaptable, Good time to market throughout its whole life cycle\", where \"Maintainable -- Complexity has to be controlled at all times and grow deliberately, not organically\", \"Scalable -- The system has to grow in data and data concepts without affecting maintainability\", \"Adaptable -- The system has to handle change of requirements (internal/external, large/small) without affecting maintainability\", \"Good time to market -- ... Not the fastest way, but always within acceptable timing\", and \"Throughout its whole life cycle -- Estimated at 15-25 years.\"",
        "layer": "process",
        "implication": "Design decisions are judged against a 15-25 year horizon, and speed is explicitly not the top criterion — 'the fastest way' is rejected in favour of acceptable timing that upholds the principles. Growth in data volume AND in number of data concepts must not degrade maintainability, which bounds how much bespoke, hand-written per-source or per-report code the platform may accumulate."
      },
      {
        "claim": "When a requirement does not fit, the blueprint is used to cost the deviation, not to block it: \"we do not argue with the reality, we define the cost that the reality wants to impose on us\" and \"The gatekeeper does not say \"no\" -- it says \"let's understand what this costs us before we decide.\"\" The listed questions include \"What time and cost to define and integrate the data needed into DAB?\" and \"What future value would integrating the data into DAB first give our company and the system?\"",
        "layer": "process",
        "implication": "Set up an explicit deviation path: when a requirement forces a break, record which principle is broken, why, how, the effect on maintainability/scalability/adaptability/time-to-market, the reusability lost by short-circuiting the architecture, and the future life-cycle cost — with the product owner, requirement owner, data warehouse architect and data engineer all party to the decision."
      }
    ],
