    "thesis": "Operational systems each own their own identifiers and never have to agree; the moment data is brought together for analytics, that multiplicity becomes the problem of representing one real instance as one instance. The post's answer is the IDFR pattern from the Focal Framework: turn each business identifier into a globally unique, cradle-to-grave \"analytical identifier\" by prefixing its raw value with a fixed-width Owner+Type code ({Owner}{Type}{Value}, e.g. 4+4 chars, concatenated without a separator), then load every such identifier into a generic per-entity `{ENTITY}_idfr` table that maps many identifiers to one surrogate key. A four-stage pipeline (READER → INTEGRATION → TRUEDELTA → INSERT) LEFT JOINs each of up to five incoming identifier slots against the entire `_idfr` table by value (not by slot position), reuses the matched surrogate key when any slot matches, and mints a fresh UUID when none does. Integration therefore happens at design time in the DAB layer as a property of the identifier itself — not as a downstream reconciliation step, and unlike Data Vault's same-as-link, which defers the reconciliation rather than removing it.",
